{{!-- The masthead strip: a chip per writer, with their post count.

	{{> "writers" anchor="authors" headingId="au-head"}}   the homepage's
	{{> "writers" anchor="writers" headingId="wr-head"}}    the Feed's

	anchor / headingId    the ids the design gives the strip on each Surface, because a link on
	                      the same page reaches it and two Surfaces must not share one id

Ten at most, ordered by post count. The design draws every writer because its demo publication
has five; a real masthead has no bound, and the Authors Directory is the Surface that lists all
of them. The grid auto-fills, so any number between two and ten fills its rows.

The face is post/avatar.hbs, the same drawing the reading Surface uses: a writer who uploaded a
photo gets the photo, and the tinted initial is the fallback. The design draws a two-letter
monogram everywhere; that gap is one concession, recorded once, and not re-decided here.

A STAFF ACCOUNT IS NOT YET A WRITER, AND ONE WRITER IS NOT A MASTHEAD. Every staff user is an
"author" to Ghost, so an owner or an editor who has not published renders as a chip reading "No
posts" — on this install that was a sixth chip beside the design's five. And a publication with a
single writer has no masthead to draw: one chip under the word "Writers" states what every byline
on the page already said, so the whole section is withheld until there are two.

Both tests are `writers.[N].count.posts` rather than a filter or a count, because count.posts is
NOT filterable on authors in Ghost 6: `filter="count.posts:>0"` returns an empty set and
`filter="posts.count:>0"` fails the query outright, and Handlebars cannot compare a total to 2.
Ordering by count.posts descending is what makes an index enough: the SECOND row carries the
second-highest count, so if it has published then at least two writers have. The per-chip guard in
the loop stays — it is what drops the seventh staff account from a strip of six writers.

`../anchor` and `../headingId`, not the bare names: {{#get}} renders its block with the query's
response as the context, so a partial argument passed to THIS partial is one frame up from inside
the block. Read bare they are undefined and the section ships `id=""` and an `aria-labelledby`
pointing at nothing — a region landmark with no accessible name, and the anchor a link on the same
page is supposed to reach. --}}
{{#get "authors" include="count.posts" order="count.posts desc" limit="10" as |writers|}}
	{{#if writers.[1].count.posts}}
		<section
			id="{{../anchor}}"
			aria-labelledby="{{../headingId}}"
			class="container pt-16">
			<h2
				id="{{../headingId}}"
				class="font-ui text-muted mb-6 text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.16em] uppercase">{{t "Writers"}}</h2>

			<div class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(210px,100%),1fr))] gap-4">
				{{#foreach writers}}
					{{#if count.posts}}
						<a
							href="{{url}}"
							class="border-border bg-surface text-text hover:border-accent flex items-center gap-3.5 rounded-md border p-4 no-underline">
							{{> "post/avatar" size="size-[42px]" textSize="text-sm"}}

							<span class="flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-[3px]">
								<span class="font-ui text-[14.5px] font-semibold">{{name}}</span>
								<span class="text-muted font-mono text-[11px]">{{plural count.posts empty=(t "No posts") singular=(t "% post") plural=(t "% posts")}}</span>
							</span>
						</a>
					{{/if}}
				{{/foreach}}
			</div>
		</section>
	{{/if}}
{{/get}}
